  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Little change in the weather pattern forecast\ntoday as high pressure and dry airmass remains in place and\ntemperatures only slightly below normal for early October.\nRadiational cooling occurring this hour with some of the more\nreliably cool spots already into the upper 40s. Low-level\nthicknesses slowly build today and highs will be a few degrees\nwarmer than yesterday but still below 80. A modest uptick in TDs\nsuggests lows overnight in the low to mid 50s, but well\nsheltered spots could make a run again into the upper 40s.\n\nOverall airmass will be too dry for widespread fog, but some\nvery shallow and transient patches are possible this morning and\nnext.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Overall pattern evolves later this week as upper\nlevel ridge expands across the southeastern CONUS in response to\ndeepening troughing over the Rio Grande Valley as well as a\ndeveloping tropical cyclone (currently designated TD 9 and\nreferred to as such for the remainder of the discussion) over \nthe south- central Gulf. Conditions remain dry over the \nCarolinas, but the rising low- level thicknesses point to highs \nrebounding to above normal. Most inland areas will reach the low\nto mid 80s, with highs around 80 near-shore. \n\nBy the weekend, TD 9 is forecast to have strengthened and lifted\nnorthward towards the Gulf Coast, interacting with the mid-level\ntrough. This evolution is complex and model guidance still shows\nsome spread on its timing and track, especially as it migrates\nfurther inland. What is likely is east of TD 9 a warm front will\nlift northeast across the southeastern CONUS on the leading edge\nof the cyclone's deep tropical moisture. The potential for a \nwave of low pressure to develop along the front remains a low\nprobability event based on 00z guidance trends, but given the\naforementioned uncertainty trends will continue to be monitored.\n\nHighest confidence remains around a moderate to heavy rainfall\nevent across eastern NC. Timing of rainfall onset is a source of\nconsiderable uncertainty, with this morning's guidance suite now\nfavoring a Saturday afternoon/evening and Sunday time frame. The\nAI guidance and the GFS have shifted more into this faster camp,\nwhile the ECMWF model remains slower (its AI counterpart,\ninterestingly, favors a faster onset as well). Given this\nnotable change, WPC has placed much of our area in a Marginal (Level\n1 of 4) risk of excessive rainfall covering Sat AM to Sun AM. \nThe probability for exceeding an inch of rain across all \nensemble suites, regardless of timing, still remains around the \n50-75% for much of the area. Probabilities of seeing rainfall\nexceeding 3\" are now up to 25-30%, an increase from yesterday.\n\nGuidance suggests minimal instability, but sometimes this type \nof setup can favor some shallow, rotating thunderstorms along \nthe advancing warm front where southeasterly flow is enhanced.",
